# Credentials. Never commit these.
.env

# Byte-compiled / cache
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Virtualenv
.venv/
venv/

# Vector store and logs are build artifacts, not source.
chroma_db/
data/logs/

# data/processed/vector_index/ is deliberately NOT ignored - see the note below.
# The index is a build artifact, but on Render it is also the reason the free
# instance can start at all: the 720-chunk build peaks at 565 MB (fp32, batch 8)
# against a 512 MB limit, so an instance that builds on boot is killed before it
# serves a question. Committing the 2.8 MB index moves that work to a developer
# machine, where a full rebuild costs 2.2 minutes and no cold start is at risk.
# Render then only *loads* it (489 MB resident), which fits.
#
# The trade: the committed vectors can drift from data/raw. The manifest carries
# corpus_version and embed_model, and localindex rebuilds on either mismatch, so a
# stale index self-heals rather than silently answering from old facts.
# Rebuild with: python -m scripts.build_index

# models/ is deliberately NOT ignored either, and it is the bigger of the two.
# Committing the vector index removed the *build-time* need to download the
# embedding model, but serving a question still needs it in the process -
# app/retrieve calls embed_query() on every question. Render's filesystem starts
# empty, so leaving the weights on the hub meant an ~87 MB download from
# huggingface.co on every cold start and every spin-down. That host is
# Cloudflare-fronted and answers past its quota with HTTP 429 plus a "Just a
# moment..." HTML page, which surfaced as a LIVE service whose chat box reported a
# connection error. Every cold start raced the same limit from a shared free-tier
# IP, so retrying and HF_TOKEN did not fix it.
#
# The weights are the same MiniLM the vectors were built with - re-embedding a
# spread of chunks from models/ reproduces vectors.npy to 5.96e-08, which is
# float32 epsilon - so the index above is unaffected and only its manifest label
# changed. Refresh with: python -m scripts.vendor_model

# Audit dumps are regenerable from data/raw via scripts/.
data/processed/chunks.txt
data/processed/embeddings.txt
data/processed/ingest_report.json
